This version incorporates the mathematical and methodological audit of v0.6. Principal changes: (i) H1 now includes a sensitivity analysis protocol for unmeasured confounding, a total error decomposition separating model error from Monte Carlo proxy error, a tiered MC sampling scheme replacing the infeasible uniform bound, and a simulator invariance audit; (ii) the provenance kernel now specifies both soundness and completeness invariants, with sorry-free obligations or explicit axiom declaration under a declared trusted base, and the H4 benchmark inherits an independent-generation and power-analysis requirement; (iii) the CBF kernel is strengthened to a forward-invariance theorem with class-\mathcal{K}_\infty axioms stated formally, and a sampled-data scope assumption is made explicit; (iv) H5 replaces AURC-as-primary with a two-part criterion: a monotonicity gate followed by excess-AURC conditioned on gate passage; (v) the hierarchical model is augmented with a minimum cluster count, a Bayesian fallback, and a GLMM extension for non-Gaussian outcomes; (vi) negative controls are operationalized with pre-registered tolerances and halt rules; (vii) a formal–empirical separation principle is stated so that Lean kernels constrain specifications, not implementations. No empirical claims are made.

---

Abstract

Autonomous learning systems increasingly couple predictive representation learning, action-conditioned world models, memory, and planning. The governing research question is not whether a predictive representation can support action, but under what formal conditions it supports reliable intervention-sensitive prediction, calibrated abstention, and accountable knowledge revision.

This paper specifies a Phase I benchmark protocol for three interfaces: intervention-contrast prediction (H1), provenance-aware invalidation (H4), and uncertainty-guided abstention (H5). The protocol is grounded in a structural causal model with explicit conditional exchangeability assumptions and a sensitivity analysis that converts an untestable identification assumption into a measurable degradation profile; a total error decomposition separating model estimation from Monte Carlo proxy error; a tiered Monte Carlo scheme that preserves finite-sample guarantees where they matter; a type-theoretic provenance graph with both soundness and completeness invariants; a discrete Control Barrier Function shield with an explicit forward-invariance theorem and a sampled-data scope assumption; and a hierarchical linear mixed-effects model with a minimum cluster count, a Bayesian fallback, and a GLMM extension.

A formal–empirical separation principle is stated: Lean 4 kernels constrain the specified transformation, not the implemented system. The contribution is a reproducible, falsifiable specification. It is not a claim that the architecture has been demonstrated.

---

1. Introduction and positioning

A learning system can predict observations accurately without identifying the mechanisms that generate them. It may anticipate likely transitions or produce plausible future representations while remaining unreliable when an action changes the environment in an unfamiliar way.

For autonomous systems, this distinction is decisive. An agent that only predicts what usually happens may perform well in familiar conditions but fail when it must choose between alternative interventions, detect environmental change, or determine whether its own knowledge remains valid.

The paper's contribution is deliberately narrow. It specifies a Phase I benchmark programme for testing whether three interfaces improve autonomous decision-making under distribution shift and resource constraints:

· prediction under alternative interventions;
· abstention when evidence is insufficient;
· provenance-aware invalidation of reusable knowledge.

The guiding operational principle is: abstain when evidence is insufficient; experiment when the expected information value justifies the cost; preserve the evidence trail when knowledge changes.

The paper is a specification and protocol, not a report of achieved performance.

