2. Formal setting

2.1 Structural causal model

Let the underlying structural causal model (SCM) be defined over latent state S_t, action A_t, and environment variable \mathcal{E}_t:

S_{t+1} = f_s(S_t, A_t, U_{s,t}), \qquad O_t = f_o(S_t, U_{o,t}),

where U_{s,t} and U_{o,t} are exogenous unobserved random variables. The agent receives O_t, selects A_t \in \mathcal{A}, and maintains H_t = f_\psi(H_{t-1}, O_t, A_{t-1}, M_t) with representation Z_t = E_\theta(H_t).

2.2 Conditional exchangeability and identifiability

For the conditional intervention contrast

\tau(s,a,a') = \mathbb{E}\!\left[Y(a) - Y(a') \mid S=s\right]

to be non-parametrically identified, the protocol requires conditional exchangeability (unconfoundedness):

Y(a) \perp\!\!\!\perp A \mid S=s \qquad \forall a \in \mathcal{A}.

If this condition fails because of unobserved confounding, \tau(s,a,a') is not mathematically defined as a causal effect. The model output must then be reported strictly as an associative conditional expectation:

\widehat{\tau}_{\mathrm{assoc}}(s,a,a') = \mathbb{E}[Y \mid S=s, A=a] - \mathbb{E}[Y \mid S=s, A=a'].

The protocol never labels the associative quantity as a causal effect. Section 3.4 formalizes the downgrade rule and its associated endpoint so that associative estimators are not scored against causal ground truth on a common scale.

2.3 Simulator invariance audit

Before any H1 result is reported, the protocol requires a pre-registered simulator invariance audit. Let \{(s_i, a_i, u_i^{(k)})\}_{k=1}^{K} be the ground-truth draw set for a state–action pair. The audit verifies, on a pre-specified calibration set \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{cal}} \subset \mathcal{S}:

\left| \widehat{\mathrm{Cov}}\!\left( f_s(s,a,u) ,\, u \right) \right| \le \varepsilon_{\mathrm{inv}}, \qquad
\left| \frac{1}{K}\sum_{k} f_s(s_i, a_i, u_i^{(k)}) - \mu_{s_i,a_i} \right| \le \varepsilon_{\mathrm{mean}},

for a pre-registered tolerance pair (\varepsilon_{\mathrm{inv}}, \varepsilon_{\mathrm{mean}}) and a pre-registered calibration procedure for \mu_{s_i,a_i}. Failure of the audit invalidates the H1 ground truth for the affected region and must be reported. This prevents the protocol from silently assuming transition invariance across exogenous draws.

