4. H4: Provenance invalidation as a type-theoretic graph transformation

4.1 Knowledge graph

A knowledge item is K_i = (id_i, v_i, c_i, p_i, s_i, e_i, d_i, \tau_i) — persistent identifier, version, content, provenance, status, evidence, dependencies, temporal/contextual metadata. Dependencies form a directed acyclic graph G_K = (V_K, E_K).

4.2 Three distinct error sources

H4 separates three failures: (1) dependency-detection failure; (2) propagation failure; (3) reuse failure. With TP_{\mathrm{dep}}, FP_{\mathrm{dep}}, FN_{\mathrm{dep}}:

P_{\mathrm{dep}} = \frac{TP_{\mathrm{dep}}}{TP_{\mathrm{dep}}+FP_{\mathrm{dep}}}, \qquad R_{\mathrm{dep}} = \frac{TP_{\mathrm{dep}}}{TP_{\mathrm{dep}}+FN_{\mathrm{dep}}},

and the stale-reuse rate is

R_{\mathrm{stale}} = \frac{N_{\mathrm{stale\ reuse}}}{N_{\mathrm{eligible\ retrievals}}}.

4.3 Soundness and completeness of invalidation propagation

Let \mathcal{I} \subseteq V_K be the set of invalidated root claims. The propagation operator must satisfy two invariants.

Soundness. For every descendant reachable via active dependency paths,

\forall K_j \in \operatorname{Descendants}(G_K,\mathcal{I}), \qquad \operatorname{Status}(K_j) \in \{\text{Invalidated}, \text{Reexamine}\}.

Completeness. An item that does not depend on any invalidated root must not be spuriously invalidated or re-examined:

\forall K_j \in V_K,\ \forall K_i \in \mathcal{I}, \quad \neg\,\operatorname{DependsTransitive}(G_K, K_i, K_j) \implies \operatorname{Status}(K_j) = \text{Valid}.

Soundness alone is trivially satisfiable by an operator that invalidates everything; completeness prevents this degenerate implementation. A dependency edge does not imply that every descendant conclusion is false: the protocol distinguishes Invalidated descendants (evidential support removed) from Reexamine descendants (support must be reviewed but the claim is not declared false).

4.4 Formal verification kernel

The provenance kernel is formalized in Lean 4 using a finite-set graph representation with a KnowledgeStatus inductive type, a ProvenanceGraph structure with a well-formedness invariant, a transitive dependency predicate, and a propagation operator. The verified obligations are:

Theorem (H4 soundness). For any well-formed G, any \mathcal{I}, and any K_j with \operatorname{DependsTransitive}(G, K_i, K_j) for some K_i \in \mathcal{I}, the propagated status of K_j is not Valid.

Theorem (H4 completeness). For any well-formed G, any \mathcal{I}, and any K_j with \neg\operatorname{DependsTransitive}(G, K_i, K_j) for all K_i \in \mathcal{I}, the propagated status of K_j is Valid.

Sorry-free obligation. The kernel must either complete the structural-preservation lemmas (e.g., invariance of wellFormed under the image of a status update, which follows by Finset induction) or declare them as explicit axioms within a declared trusted base. The protocol reports, for every theorem, the transitive closure of axioms on which its proof depends against the declared trusted set. A theorem is certified iff it type-checks against the Lean kernel and its axiom closure is contained in the trusted base. The phrase "routine but lengthy" is not a formal justification and is disallowed.

4.5 Benchmark scale, independence, and power

The H4 experiment requires a benchmark of at least 1{,}000 claims with dependency graphs of depth \ge 5, independently generated (not hand-curated by the protocol authors) from a documented generative model. A power analysis is pre-registered for detecting a 10\% reduction in R_{\mathrm{stale}} at \alpha=0.01 with power 1-\beta=0.80. The protocol reports the achieved sample size relative to the pre-registered requirement. This addresses the well-documented limitation that dependency-aware invalidation frameworks (e.g., the PACMI family) are typically validated on diagnostic benchmarks of order 10^2 cases with correspondingly limited power, and that their reported advantages over strong baselines can fail to reach significance under ablation.

4.6 H4 comparison and negative control

H4 compares a dependency-aware registry with a flat registry on R_{\mathrm{stale}}, while separately reporting P_{\mathrm{dep}} and R_{\mathrm{dep}}. The negative control is a model that retains invalidated facts in its parameters even when the registry flags them. H4 is not supported if R_{\mathrm{stale}} is not reduced under matched retrieval and latency budgets.

