7. Matched-budget comparison rules

7.1 Required budget disclosure

Every method comparison reports: training data (exact transition/episode count), validation data (exact count), interventions executed (exact experiment count), training compute (GPU-hours/FLOPs), inference latency (ms per decision, full distribution), memory (parameters/GB), tuning trials (exact configuration count), and environment access (binary and count for privileged state and simulator calls). A method that uses more data or an order of magnitude more compute cannot be declared superior without making that difference explicit.

7.2 Matching rule

For H1, H4, and H5, the proposed method and the strongest baseline are matched on training data, intervention budget, and training compute. If exact matching is impossible, the protocol reports the ratio and performs a budget-sensitivity analysis.

7.3 Fair comparison statement

The protocol does not claim that a mechanism is useful if its apparent advantage disappears under matched budgets. The negative-result branch is an acceptable and informative outcome.

