9. Phase I experimental package

Experiment 1 — H1: Intervention-contrast benchmark

Inputs. Resettable initial states, randomized interventions, tiered Monte Carlo draws (calibration tier K_{\mathrm{cal}}, certification tier K), held-out intervention combinations, and a pre-registered simulator invariance audit on \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{cal}}.

Outputs. E_{\mathrm{CATE}} or E_{\mathrm{assoc}} (per the downgrade rule), E_{\mathrm{worst}}, E_{\mathrm{marginal}}, latent contrast diagnostics, the sensitivity curve \beta(\gamma), the plug-in bias B_{\mathrm{plugin}} on \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{analytic}}, the exact K used per pair and |\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{cert}}|, and the Monte Carlo bound.

Baselines. Action-free predictor, action-conditioned predictor, object-masking predictor, oracle-simulator diagnostic (reported as an upper bound, not as a competitive baseline).

Negative control. Intervention objective without intervention coverage, with a pre-registered expected failure signature (§11).

Experiment 2 — H4: Knowledge-invalidation benchmark

Inputs. \ge 1{,}000 claims with dependency graphs of depth \ge 5, independently generated; controlled invalidations, supersessions, scope changes, contradictory evidence.

Outputs. P_{\mathrm{dep}}, R_{\mathrm{dep}}, R_{\mathrm{stale}}, false-invalidation rate, time-to-detection, latency, and the achieved power for detecting a 10\% reduction in R_{\mathrm{stale}}.

Baselines. Unstructured retrieval, versioned memory without propagation, versioned memory with propagation, full registry with retrieval-time conflict checks.

Negative control. Model retains invalidated facts in parameters.

Experiment 3 — H5: Selective-prediction benchmark

Inputs. Predictions, uncertainty signals, shifted distributions, CBF shield configuration, and a pre-registered monotonicity tolerance \epsilon_{\mathrm{mono}}.

Outputs. Monotonicity gate outcome, \operatorname{AURC}_{\mathrm{excess}} (conditional on gate), R_{\mathrm{sel}}(\phi) at pre-registered coverage, coverage, abstention rate, R_{\mathrm{harm}}, calibration (ECE and proper scoring rules).

Baselines. Softmax confidence, temperature scaling, ensemble, epinet-style estimator, novelty baseline.

Negative control. Confidently wrong estimator with shield disabled.

9.1 Independence and integration

The three experiments are specified independently so that a failure in one does not invalidate the others. Integration is a later-phase question. No Phase I claim depends on all three succeeding.

