10. Operationalized negative controls and broken-world tests

Each negative control is pre-registered with a metric, an expected direction of degradation, a tolerance interval, and a halt rule. The table below specifies the tolerance \tau_{\mathrm{neg}} and halt rule.

Claim Negative control Expected signature Tolerance and halt rule
H1 conditional contrast No intervention coverage E_{\mathrm{CATE}} does not improve Halt if the negative control improves E_{\mathrm{CATE}} by more than \tau_{\mathrm{neg}} over the action-conditioned baseline
H1 unconfoundedness Confounder injected (\gamma) Causal claim downgrades Downgrade to E_{\mathrm{assoc}} when \|\beta(\gamma)\| > \epsilon_\tau for the smallest plausible \gamma
H4 dependency detection Flat registry P_{\mathrm{dep}}, R_{\mathrm{dep}} collapse Halt if P_{\mathrm{dep}} or R_{\mathrm{dep}} exceeds \tau_{\mathrm{neg}} under flat registry
H4 reuse Model retains invalidated facts R_{\mathrm{stale}} remains high despite correct flags Halt if R_{\mathrm{stale}} drops below \tau_{\mathrm{neg}} under the retaining model
H5 selective risk Confidently wrong estimator AURC improves while accepted harmful errors persist Halt if \operatorname{AURC}_{\mathrm{excess}} drops below \tau_{\mathrm{neg}} under the confidently-wrong estimator
H5 operational risk Shield disabled R_{\mathrm{harm}} exceeds budget Halt if R_{\mathrm{harm}} \le \epsilon_{\mathrm{harm}} with shield disabled
H5 CBF soundness Barrier misspecified Forward invariance violated Halt if the forward-invariance test set is non-empty

A negative control that unexpectedly passes (i.e., does not exhibit the expected degradation) does not "support" the claim under test; it triggers a mandatory review of the control's specification. The protocol reports the outcome of every negative control before any positive result.

