11. Threats to validity

11.1 Predictive accuracy without intervention competence. A model may predict observed transitions while failing to distinguish alternative interventions. Conditional contrasts are evaluated separately from ordinary prediction error.

11.2 Latent separability without causal correctness. Latent separability is necessary for some forms of action discrimination but not sufficient for causal correctness; it is reported only as a diagnostic.

11.3 Unconfoundedness violations. When conditional exchangeability fails, the protocol does not report a causal effect. It reports an associative contrast, labels the result accordingly, and does not score associative estimators against causal ground truth on a common scale (§3.4).

11.4 Uncertainty miscalibration and misspecification. A model may be confident under shift, and a single scalar score does not identify aleatoric variability, parameter uncertainty, misspecification, and distribution shift simultaneously. Conformal coverage under exchangeability is not an OOD guarantee.

11.5 Provenance is not truthfulness. A registry tracks explicit claims. It cannot guarantee that a neural model will not regenerate an invalidated claim from parameters. The registry is evaluated as an evidence-management mechanism, not as a universal truth filter.

11.6 Correlated multi-agent error. Agreement among agents trained on the same data is not independent verification. Deferred to later phases but noted because it constrains the interpretation of cumulative-learning claims.

11.7 Benchmark overfitting. A method may optimize the measured benchmark while exploiting evaluation weaknesses. Held-out environments, adversarial tests, and pre-registered endpoints are required.

11.8 Simulator model misspecification. If the simulator violates the invariance audit (§2.3), the ground-truth contrast is not well-defined for the affected region and the H1 result must be reported as inconclusive in that region.

11.9 Small-cluster bias. When the number of environments is below the minimum cluster count (§6.3), REML-based inference can be anti-conservative. The protocol mandates the Bayesian and cluster-robust sensitivity analyses.

11.10 Formal–empirical gap. See §12.

---

12. Formal–empirical separation principle

The protocol combines machine-checkable formal kernels with empirical benchmark claims. These two modes of inquiry have different notions of validity:

· A Lean theorem is valid if it type-checks against the kernel—it is absolute within the trusted base, but says nothing about whether the formalization matches the intended real-world concept.
· An empirical benchmark is valid if it is reproducible, powered, and robust to distribution shift—it is always provisional and context-dependent.

The protocol therefore states the following separation principle.

Formal–empirical separation principle. A Lean 4 kernel constrains the specified transformation, not the implemented system. A kernel proof establishes that the specification satisfies its stated invariants; it does not establish that an implementation instantiates the specification, nor that the specification corresponds to the intended real-world mechanism.

The implications are threefold. First, the formal kernels are specification debugging tools: they force the protocol to state its invariants precisely and expose hidden assumptions (e.g., the class-\mathcal{K}_\infty axioms on \alpha, the completeness invariant on invalidation). Second, the formal kernels are not evidence that an implemented retrieval system, CBF shield, or contrast estimator satisfies the corresponding invariant; empirical verification of implementation conformance to the specification is a separate obligation. Third, implementation conformance tests must be pre-registered and reported alongside the kernel proofs. For H4, this means a differential test comparing the implemented propagation operator against the Lean-specified operator on a randomized corpus. For H5, this means a forward-invariance test in the simulator comparing the observed successor states against the CBF predicate. A method whose kernel proof passes but whose conformance test fails is reported as non-conforming, not as a success.

