14. Conclusion

This paper specifies a Phase I benchmark protocol for testing three interfaces that are often evaluated separately: whether a system predicts the consequences of alternative actions, whether it declines to act when evidence is insufficient, and whether it prevents invalidated knowledge from governing its decisions.

The protocol's methodological commitments are: conditional intervention contrasts under explicit unconfoundedness assumptions, with a sensitivity analysis that converts the assumption into a measurable degradation profile; finite-sample Monte Carlo bounds complemented by a total error decomposition and a tiered sampling scheme; decomposed provenance errors with machine-checkable soundness and completeness; separated predictive and operational risk with a monotonicity gate preceding AURC; a control-theoretic safety shield with an explicit forward-invariance theorem and a sampled-data scope assumption; hierarchical treatment of correlated observations with REML estimation, minimum cluster counts, and Bayesian and GLMM fallbacks; matched budgets for every comparison; operationalized negative controls with tolerance intervals and halt rules; and a formal–empirical separation principle that constrains the reach of the Lean kernels to specifications rather than implementations.

The paper does not claim that the architecture has been demonstrated. It claims that the protocol is specific enough to be implemented, falsified, and compared across research groups. The first public artifact should include the simulator, data-generation code, baseline implementations, pre-registration, Lean 4 kernels with axiom audits, evaluation scripts, conformance tests, full logs, and failure cases.

A system should not be judged only by how well it predicts what comes next. It should also be judged by whether it knows when its predictions are insufficient, whether it can learn from controlled experiments, and whether it can preserve the evidence behind what it has learned.


