Appendix C. Simulator invariance audit

C.1 Hypotheses

For a state–action pair (s,a), let Y_k = Y(s,a;\omega_k), k = 1,\dots,K, be draws under exogenous randomness \omega_k, with exogenous covariate U_k recorded by the simulator. The audit tests two null hypotheses on a calibration set \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{cal}}:

H_0^{\mathrm{exo}} : \operatorname{Cov}\bigl(Y, U\bigr) = 0,
\qquad
H_0^{\mathrm{mean}} : \Bigl| K^{-1}\textstyle\sum_{k} Y_k - \mu_{s,a}\Bigr| \le \varepsilon_{\mathrm{mean}},

where \mu_{s,a} is an independently computed reference mean (analytic, or from a disjoint high-K reference run).

C.2 Statistics and decision rule

\widehat{\rho}(s,a) = \frac{\widehat{\operatorname{Cov}}(Y, U)}{\widehat{\sigma}_Y \widehat{\sigma}_U},
\qquad
\widehat{w}(s,a) = z_{1-\alpha_{\mathrm{audit}}/2}\,\widehat{\sigma}_Y\, K^{-1/2}.

The audit passes at (s,a) iff |\widehat{\rho}(s,a)| \le \varepsilon_{\mathrm{inv}} and \widehat{w}(s,a) \le \varepsilon_{\mathrm{mean}}. The tolerance pair (\varepsilon_{\mathrm{inv}}, \varepsilon_{\mathrm{mean}}) is pre-registered, and \alpha_{\mathrm{audit}} is corrected across pairs by Holm–Bonferroni. The audit is reported per region, since failure is typically localized. A failed region is excluded from H1 reporting, and the exclusion fraction is disclosed alongside every H1 result; if the excluded fraction exceeds a pre-registered ceiling \eta_{\mathrm{audit}}, the H1 experiment is declared invalid rather than partially reported.

C.3 Rationale

Without the audit, the protocol's central ground-truth object \tau^{\mathrm{GT}} is defined with respect to exogenous draws whose invariance is merely asserted. The audit converts that assertion into a falsifiable test with an explicit multiplicity correction and an explicit invalidation rule.

---

Appendix D. Sensitivity analysis: analytic bias curve and downgrade rule

D.1 Linear-Gaussian calibration model

On the calibration set \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{conf}}, the simulator is augmented with a scalar unmeasured confounder U \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1) and

A = \theta U + \varepsilon_A, \quad \varepsilon_A \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_A^2),
\qquad
Y = \tau A + \delta U + \varepsilon_Y, \quad \varepsilon_Y \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_Y^2),

with U \perp \varepsilon_A \perp \varepsilon_Y. The naive least-squares slope has expectation

\mathbb{E}\bigl[\widehat{\tau}_{\mathrm{naive}}\bigr]
= \tau + \frac{\delta\theta}{\theta^2 + \sigma_A^2}
=: \tau + \beta(\gamma),
\qquad \gamma := \delta\theta.

The bias is therefore

\beta(\gamma) = \frac{\gamma}{\theta^2 + \sigma_A^2},

which is monotone in \gamma and analytic. The grid \Gamma = \{\gamma_1,\dots,\gamma_n\} is calibrated by inverting this expression so that \beta spans [0, \epsilon_\tau] in equal increments, where \epsilon_\tau is the pre-registered tolerance. This produces a bias curve whose scale is interpretable and whose shape is not an artifact of arbitrary grid choice.

D.2 Nonlinear extension

For nonlinear simulators, \beta(\gamma) is estimated by simulation with the same grid and reported with bootstrap intervals. The analytic case is retained as a calibration check: the estimated curve must agree with the analytic curve within the bootstrap interval on the linear-Gaussian sub-simulator, or the injection mechanism is reported as defective.

D.3 Downgrade rule (binding)

Let \gamma^\star be the smallest confounding strength deemed plausible by the protocol's domain model, pre-registered before any H1 run. The estimand is causal, and E_{\mathrm{CATE}} is reported, iff

\bigl|\beta(\gamma^\star)\bigr| \le \epsilon_\tau.

Otherwise the reported estimand is the associative contrast, the primary endpoint is E_{\mathrm{assoc}} of §3.4, and the causal endpoint is reported only in a separate table labelled not identified. No table reports E_{\mathrm{CATE}} and E_{\mathrm{assoc}} as comparable quantities.

---

E.1 Tiered Monte Carlo: cost, selection bias, and guarantee preservation

E.1 Split-sample requirement

Selecting the certification set \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{cert}} = \{(s,a,a') : w_{\mathrm{cal}} > \epsilon\} on the basis of estimated variance introduces a selection effect: the calibration estimate that triggers certification is positively correlated with the calibration estimate of the contrast, so reusing the calibration draws for certification biases the certified ground truth. The protocol therefore mandates disjoint seeds:

\omega^{(1)}_{1..K_{\mathrm{cal}}} \ \text{(selection only)},
\qquad
\omega^{(2)}_{1..K} \ \text{(certification only)}.

The Hoeffding guarantee then applies to \widehat{\tau}^{\mathrm{GT}} computed from \omega^{(2)} alone, since the conditioning on \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{cert}} depends only on \omega^{(1)}.

E.2 Cost

C_{\mathrm{total}} = K_{\mathrm{cal}}\,|\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{eval}}| + K\,|\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{cert}}|,

with expected value under state-heterogeneous variance \mathbb{E}[C_{\mathrm{total}}] = K_{\mathrm{cal}}|\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{eval}}| + K\,\mathbb{E}[|\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{cert}}|]. The protocol reports |\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{cert}}|, the realized cost, and the ratio C_{\mathrm{total}} / (K|\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{eval}}|).

E.3 Coverage-guarantee caveat

The bound is per-pair, not simultaneous over \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{eval}}. A simultaneous statement requires a union bound: to guarantee \epsilon for all pairs in \mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{eval}} at level \alpha, use \alpha/|\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{eval}}| in the sample-size formula. The protocol reports both the per-pair bound and the Bonferroni-corrected simultaneous bound, and declares which one underwrites each comparison.

E.4 Selection power

The scheme's efficiency depends on the calibration tier separating high- from low-variance pairs. The protocol reports the empirical ROC of the calibration statistic w_{\mathrm{cal}} against the realized certification-tier half-width, so that readers can assess whether the tiering is informative on the given simulator.

---

F.1 H4 benchmark: generative model, sample size, power

F.1.1 Generative model for dependency graphs

Graphs are sampled from a layered directed acyclic model with parameters \pi_{\mathrm{fork}} (probability of branching), \pi_{\mathrm{merge}} (probability of convergence), depth d \ge 5, and width w, with a pre-registered random seed and no manual curation. Each claim is generated with a content template and a ground-truth validity label; invalidations are applied to a random subset of roots with contamination levels \rho \in \{0.05, 0.10, 0.20\}.

F.1.2 Power for R_{\mathrm{stale}}

For a two-proportion comparison of stale-reuse rates p_1 (baseline) and p_2 = (1-\delta)p_1, the required number of eligible retrievals is

n \;\ge\; \frac{\bigl(z_{1-\alpha/2} + z_{1-\beta}\bigr)^2 \bigl[p_1(1-p_1) + p_2(1-p_2)\bigr]}{(p_1 - p_2)^2},

with \alpha = 0.01, 1-\beta = 0.80, \delta = 0.10, and p_1 taken from a pilot or from the published diagnostic benchmark. Because retrievals within a claim are correlated, the effective sample size is n/\operatorname{DEFF} with design effect \operatorname{DEFF} = 1 + (\bar m - 1)\operatorname{ICC}_{\mathrm{ret}}; the protocol reports \operatorname{DEFF} and inflates the target accordingly.

F.1.3 Scale floor

The claim set is floored at 1{,}000 items with depth \ge 5 and at least 30 independent roots per invalidation batch. If the power calculation exceeds the floor, the floor is raised, not lowered.

---

G.1 Hierarchical inference: GLMM, priors, sandwich, calibration

G.1.1 Linear model and REML

D_{e,s} = \mathbf{x}_{e,s}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\beta} + u_e + v_{s(e)} + \varepsilon_{e,s},
\quad
u_e \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_u^2),\;
v_{s(e)} \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_v^2),\;
\varepsilon \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_\varepsilon^2).

Variance components by REML; contrasts tested by Wald statistics with Kenward–Roger or Satterthwaite denominator degrees of freedom.

G.1.2 GLMM for non-Gaussian outcomes

For a rate, count, or bounded outcome, the protocol pre-specifies

g\bigl(\mathbb{E}[D_{e,s} \mid u_e, v_{s(e)}]\bigr) = \mathbf{x}_{e,s}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\beta} + u_e + v_{s(e)},

with g the logit for rates and proportions, the log for counts, and the probit for ordinal bounded scores. The marginal likelihood is approximated by Laplace's method; the fit is reported with the approximation error estimated by a parametric bootstrap over B = 1{,}000 replicate datasets.

G.1.3 Small-cluster safeguards

If E < 30: (i) a Bayesian fit with half-Cauchy priors of pre-registered scale on \sigma_u, \sigma_v, \sigma_\varepsilon, reporting the 95% posterior interval; (ii) a cluster-robust sandwich variance estimator with the small-sample correction of Bell–McCaffrey; (iii) a bootstrap-t interval with cluster resampling. The confirmatory criterion of §6.4 must hold under all three analyses; disagreement is reported as inconclusive, never as a positive result.

G.1.4 Calibration check

For the GLMM, the protocol reports the coverage of the nominal 95% interval over the parametric bootstrap replicates; nominal coverage below 0.925 triggers a switch to the bootstrap percentile interval for confirmatory purposes.

---

H.1 Monotonicity gate and excess-AURC

H.1.1 Gate statistic

For a coverage grid \Phi = \{\phi_1 < \cdots < \phi_m\} with \phi_1 at full coverage and \phi_m near zero,

G(\Phi) = \max_{i=1}^{m-1}\bigl(R_{\mathrm{sel}}(\phi_{i+1}) - R_{\mathrm{sel}}(\phi_i)\bigr),
\qquad
\text{gate passes} \iff \widehat{G}(\Phi) \le \epsilon_{\mathrm{mono}} + z_{1-\alpha}\,\widehat{\operatorname{se}}\bigl(\widehat{G}\bigr),

with \widehat{\operatorname{se}} from a paired bootstrap over evaluation examples. The gate is one-sided: only upward violations count, since a strictly decreasing risk–coverage curve is the intended behaviour.

H.1.2 Isotonic reference

To separate miscalibration from sampling noise, the protocol additionally reports the isotonic projection \widehat{R}^{\,\mathrm{iso}}_{\mathrm{sel}}(\phi) (pool-adjacent-violators) and the gap

\Delta_{\mathrm{iso}} = \sup_{\phi \in \Phi}\bigl|\widehat{R}_{\mathrm{sel}}(\phi) - \widehat{R}^{\,\mathrm{iso}}_{\mathrm{sel}}(\phi)\bigr|.

A large \Delta_{\mathrm{iso}} with a passing gate indicates local noise rather than a structural defect; a large \Delta_{\mathrm{iso}} with a failing gate indicates genuine non-monotonicity.

H.1.3 Excess-AURC

\operatorname{AURC}_{\mathrm{excess}} = \operatorname{AURC} - \operatorname{AURC}_{\mathrm{oracle}},

where \operatorname{AURC}_{\mathrm{oracle}} is computed with the ground-truth ordering of examples by their per-example loss (an oracle that knows which points it would err on). Both terms are estimated by the trapezoidal rule on \Phi, with the bootstrap interval computed over examples. The oracle is an upper-bound reference, not a competitive baseline, and is labelled as such.

H.1.4 Reporting rule

If the gate fails, \operatorname{AURC}_{\mathrm{excess}} is not reported and the method is classified non-monotone. If the gate passes but the interval of \operatorname{AURC}_{\mathrm{excess}} overlaps zero, the method is classified indistinguishable from the best matched baseline. Only a gate pass with an interval excluding zero supports H5.

---

I.1 Implementation-conformance testing

I.1.1 Conformance relation

For H4, let \mathrm{spec} be the Lean-defined propagate operator on ProvenanceGraph, and \mathrm{impl} the production registry. Conformance on a query set Q is the predicate

\mathrm{Conf}(Q) := \forall q \in Q,\ \mathrm{impl}(q).\mathrm{status} = \mathrm{spec}(q).\mathrm{status}.

For H5, conformance is the predicate that the realized successor state satisfies the CBF guard whenever the shield reports a safe action:

\mathrm{Conf}_{\mathrm{CBF}}(Q) := \forall q \in Q,\ \mathrm{IsSafeAction}\bigl(\mathrm{cbf}, s_q, \mathrm{impl}(q)\bigr) = \mathrm{true}.

I.1.2 Statistical conformance test

Because Q is sampled, the protocol reports a one-sided exact binomial lower confidence bound on the conformance rate. With n sampled queries and f failures, the bound is the largest p such that \mathbb{P}[\mathrm{Bin}(n,p) \le f] \ge \alpha. The pre-registered floor is p \ge 0.999 over n \ge 10{,}000 randomized queries generated from the Appendix F generative model (H4) or the simulator state distribution (H5).

I.1.3 Separation of evidence

A passing kernel proof and a passing conformance test are distinct artifacts and are reported in distinct tables. A failing conformance test with a passing proof is reported as a specification–implementation divergence and blocks any claim about the implemented system, regardless of the kernel's status. This operationalizes §12.

